# MCP OAuth2 discovery: a client derives the location of the authorization server metadata from the issuer
# and looks for it at the root of the host, RFC 8414 inserting the well-known segment in front of the issuer
# path. A web application deployed under a servlet context cannot answer there, so discovery stops after the
# first step unless the container redirects such a request into the context:
#
#   /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server/clover/mcp       -> /clover/mcp/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server
#   /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/clover/mcp/all/v1  -> /clover/mcp/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/all/v1
#
# The context is taken from the request, so no context name is written here and a Server deployed as the root
# application is served as well — its base is /mcp alone. The base has to be matched as the shortest prefix
# ending in /mcp: the deprecated /mcp alias of the main endpoint puts a second /mcp behind it, and taking the
# longer of the two would send a client to a path nothing serves. Only the two OAuth2 documents are matched —
# everything else under /.well-known/ is left alone, ACME challenges included.
#
# Read by the RewriteValve, declared on the Host in server.xml, which takes its rules from
# conf/<Engine>/<Host>/rewrite.config — conf/Catalina/localhost/rewrite.config in a default installation:
#
#   <Valve className="org.apache.catalina.valves.rewrite.RewriteValve" />
#
# Host level is where it has to sit: the request never reaches a context, so a context-level valve would not run.

RewriteRule ^/\.well-known/(oauth-authorization-server|oauth-protected-resource)((?:/.+?)?/mcp)(/.+)$ $2/.well-known/$1$3 [R=302,L]
RewriteRule ^/\.well-known/(oauth-authorization-server|oauth-protected-resource)((?:/.+?)?/mcp)$ $2/.well-known/$1 [R=302,L]
